# Elm: one STRAIGHT trunk (Stems 1) whose top 65 % is a crown that FILLS an egg-shaped dome (Trunk Split 4 3 0 0.72 -0.4 1 1.8 2 - the 7th raises the MIDDLE, the 8th adds 2 leafy shoots on the inner branches,
# the 6th value = fill; Crown Radius 7.5): every split divides its parent's cone of directions among 4 children, three
# rounds, 64 tips on the dome - snaking (the trunk shape's Wobble 14), the last round arching out a little (-0.4). Every
# tip ends in a whorl of 3 leafy limbs rising at 55 degrees (Crown Top 3 0.5 55, Crown Angle 55 55 - a drooping
# species grows its limbs at Crown Angle) that arch over a little (Module Droop 60); thin leafy limbs (Module
# Radius 0.005, ~2 cm at the base) on split tips that taper only to 0.7 a round (~1.5 cm), BranchRadius 1.5: no step
# in thickness where a leafy limb meets its tip (0.012 with BranchRadius 6 put ~5 cm limbs on ~5 mm tips). Broad OVAL leaves (ClusterLeafWidth 0.55, ClusterLeafSize 0.27; narrow or small
# ones, and strong droop, read as willow). Grey-brown, deeply furrowed bark. Temperate, a little drier and cooler than
# Oak's core - Forest types make elm woods among the oaks.
